### Tested versions

Added with the new IKModifier3D in 4.6


### System information

Irrelevant

### Issue description

I have a special use case that has revealed a mistake in the logic of all IK modifier implementations that write to the `IKModifier3DSolverInfo` struct values `current_lrest` and `current_grest`. For most use cases, the standard is to not allow animations to modify IK'd bones. For ragdolls, you would use the physical skeleton modifier and set up restrictions. For active ragdolls, you would use a two-skeleton system which moves the simulated skeleton bones to the animated skeleton positions.

I have a case where the simulation is the "source of truth," i.e. the IK must operate from the result of the physics simulation, and then joint motors try to match to the IK. This requires that poses be copied from the simulation onto the skeleton using a custom modifier.

CCDIK3D treats `current_grest` as the "global rest" bone pose for imposing rotation limits on its results. However, `IterateIK3DSetting::cache_current_joint_rotations` writes `get_bone_pose()` to the `current_lrest` and then calculates the global rest from `current_lrest`.

This should already feel suspicious, as CCDIK3D interprets "global rest" to mean the same result as "skeleton->get_bone_global_rest()", but it is actually just the global pose.

I believe the mistake is not on CCDIK3D, but the correctly named and wrongly set/ used value of `current_grest` and `current_lrest`. Instead, they should store the actual skeleton global and local rest poses, and most other "incorrect" uses of the `lrest` and `grest` should be updated to use `skeleton->get_bone_pose()`, for example:

Instead of
https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/f08e003ff23a667c24c76c0193ba31a02b5dae99/scene/3d/iterate_ik_3d.h#L167-L178
```diff
- solver_info->current_lrest = p_skeleton->get_bone_pose(joints[HEAD].bone).basis.get_rotation_quaternion();
+ solver_info->current_lrest = p_skeleton->get_bone_rest(joints[HEAD].bone).basis.get_rotation_quaternion();
  solver_info->current_grest = parent_gpose * solver_info->current_lrest;
  solver_info->current_grest.normalize();
  Vector3 from = solver_info->forward_vector;
- Vector3 to = solver_info->current_grest.xform_inv(solver_info->current_vector).normalized();
+ Quaternion pose = p_skeleton->get_bone_pose(joints[HEAD].bone).basis.get_rotation_quaternion();
+ Quaternion gpose = parent_gpose * pose;
+ Vector3 to = gpose.xform_inv(solver_info->current_vector).normalized();
  Quaternion prev = solver_info->current_lpose;
  if (joint_settings[HEAD]->rotation_axis == ROTATION_AXIS_ALL) {
- 	solver_info->current_lpose = solver_info->current_lrest * get_swing(Quaternion(from, to), from);
+ 	solver_info->current_lpose = pose * get_swing(Quaternion(from, to), from);
  } else {
  	// To stabilize rotation path especially nearely 180deg.
- 	solver_info->current_lpose = solver_info->current_lrest * get_from_to_rotation_by_axis(from, to, joint_settings[HEAD]->get_rotation_axis_vector().normalized());
+ 	solver_info->current_lpose = pose * get_from_to_rotation_by_axis(from, to, joint_settings[HEAD]->get_rotation_axis_vector().normalized());
  }
```

I am seeing `current_lrest` update to the current bone pose right now, which is not at all reflective of the name of the variable. This likely resulted in a misunderstanding of what `current_grest` is supposed to be in the CCDIK3D solver, where it is used as the global bone rest offset by the parent's global pose (so that the limitation axis is relative to the parent bone). This is effectively causing joint axes to become mutable as if `mutable_bone_axes` was always enabled.

Either my suggestions should be worked into all the IK classes, or CCDIK3D must compute the relative global rest of the bone itself. I think that having `current_lrest` and `current_lpose` effectively always storing the same value is a mistake, as well as using `lrest` as if it was actually `bone_pose` instead of the `bone_rest_pose`.

